00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:36.450
Google Kubernetes Engine
is a Google Cloud Service.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.520
It's that box within a box.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.390
And then you have Kubernetes,
which is a container management

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:43.410
system.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:46.747
You might have heard, it
orchestrates containers.

00:02:46.747 --> 00:02:48.330
And typically, when
I think container,

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:49.860
I'm thinking Docker containers.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.840
And I'm thinking things like
rollback, and deployment,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.340
and scheduling, and
stuff like that.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.710
Google Kubernetes service
is a Google Cloud Service,

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.410
just like many of our
Google Cloud Services.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:09.170
You can interact with it
via the API, CLI, or UI.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:12.260
But Kubernetes is like
a heterogeneous thing.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:13.880
And when I think
about Kubernetes,

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:15.360
it has two big parts.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.300
It has the Kubernetes
master, and it

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:18.680
has the Kubernetes nodes.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:22.290
And the Kubernetes nodes are
where users run workloads.

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:23.930
And then the
Kubernetes master is

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:28.190
where we run all the little
tiny pieces that make up

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:30.860
the Kubernetes control
plane, if you will,

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:33.830
that manage Kubernetes.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:38.330
So we, Google, are in charge
of managing your master,

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:40.495
and you are in charge
of managing your nodes.

00:03:40.495 --> 00:03:41.870
And there's many
different things

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:43.140
you can do for your nodes.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.470
You can opt into
node auto-upgrade.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:48.640
But ultimately, it's
your responsibility.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.507
So after you've
created a cluster,

00:03:50.507 --> 00:03:52.590
now you have something
that you can interact with.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:55.410
And you can interact
with kubectl,

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:58.470
and you can create
pods and deployments,

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:04.128
and so on, or you
can use the API.

00:04:04.128 --> 00:04:05.670
So now, I'm going
to pass it to Zach.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:07.420
And Zach's going to
talk a little bit more

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.220
about the internals
of Kubernetes.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:11.970
ZACH LOAFMAN: Hey.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:12.512
Thanks, Josh.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:18.339
So as Josh alluded to, I'm
going to give you a little look

00:04:18.339 --> 00:04:20.215
under the hood for Kubernetes.

00:04:20.215 --> 00:04:21.519
This is super-basic.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.590
The basic Kubernetes
architecture

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:28.810
is that you have
these containers.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:32.280
As Josh said, these
are Docker containers.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:33.880
You have pods, which
are collections

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:38.380
of these containers, nodes
that those pods are running

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:41.980
on that then talk to a master,
which controls the cluster,

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:47.350
it and uses etcd as
its backing store.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:49.570
But that simplistic
view hides a fair amount

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:54.460
of complexity of things that we
manage for you under the hood.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:58.330
We run almost 50 different
Docker images on the master.

00:04:58.330 --> 00:04:59.830
This slide is just a sample.

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:04.410
They include obvious
things, like the API server.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:09.130
You might be familiar with the
Kube API server here, etcd.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:12.190
We run all of those
on top of kubelet,

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:15.490
but they also include
other things on the master,

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:18.580
like support pods for
logging, and monitoring,

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:22.030
and metrics, networking,
scheduling, et cetera.

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:27.980
We also manage the software that
runs on the nodes, including

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:31.893
the OS image in kubelet.

00:05:31.893 --> 00:05:33.560
And as part of the
master version, which

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:35.270
we'll get into in
just a little bit,

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:37.670
we manage the series of add-ons.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:40.970
This includes support for
logging and monitoring

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:46.890
of your pods and also things
like Istio and Knative as well.

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:50.000
And on the nodes is,
obviously, your application

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.438
as well running on top of
our OS image in kubelet.

00:05:55.438 --> 00:05:57.980
And now, I'm going to pass it
over to Kobi to talk about some

00:05:57.980 --> 00:05:58.820
of our challenges here.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:05:59.695
KOBI MAGNEZI: Thanks.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:05.740
So Kubernetes is indeed
a very powerful framework

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:06.550
and platform.

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:09.160
It's basically a
microservices framework

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:12.160
built on top of
microservices-- pretty awesome.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.620
But with all these large
set of building blocks,

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:17.950
we face the challenges of
coordinating and qualifying

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:19.750
different releases.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:21.580
Going through these
specific challenges,

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.200
I think it will
help us understand

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:27.640
the reasons and the
drivers we have to simplify

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:30.310
the process for you guys.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:35.890
So Kubernetes communities
is a super-engage community.

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:38.340
It drives lots of code changes.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:41.440
This, in turn, translate
to quarterly releases

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:44.610
of the open source
Kubernetes version.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:48.270
On top of those
quarterly-released version,

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:52.190
there are patch versions,
which include some bug fixes.

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:56.350
And there is also
GKE patch releases,

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:58.650
which includes, basically,
the magic glue that we

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:01.620
have to integrate
Kubernetes with the GCP

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.750
infrastructure,
which then, in turn,

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:06.000
allows you to use
GCP capabilities

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.200
through Kubernetes.

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:11.970
For example, you can use GCP
preemtible VM as Preemtible VM

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:14.450
under Kubernetes cluster.

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:17.150
In this specific example, you
can see the amount of version

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:19.830
that we basically support
in the fleet right now.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:23.360
So if you open up a
gcloud command line

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:27.740
and query the least of versions
that we support, and you

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:29.780
can actually ask for
a specific region,

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:32.840
you will see what version
we support from the nodes

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:34.800
and what version we
support for the master

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:37.723
in every specific
geographical location.

00:07:37.723 --> 00:07:39.140
So that's definitely
one challenge

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:43.097
having these amount of
versions to support.

00:07:43.097 --> 00:07:44.930
The other thing that
we need to take care of

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:47.690
is the version skew policy.

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:50.540
So the open source
Kubernetes policy

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:52.550
supports two minor versions.

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:54.140
So let's take 1.14.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.090
That's the latest version.

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:59.240
When 1.14 becomes
available in the market,

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:03.910
the official support would
be for 1.13 and 1.12.

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:06.022
However, it doesn't
really end there.

00:08:06.022 --> 00:08:08.230
As you probably remember
from the explanation we just

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:11.830
got from Josh and Zach,
there are two main components

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:13.390
for every Kubernetes cluster.

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:15.580
There is the master,
and there is the node.

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:19.690
And each one of them, you
can have different versions.

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:22.240
Obviously, we encourage you
and we want our customers

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:26.440
to have the same version in
both the master and the nodes.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:29.290
But the supported
version skew allow

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:33.520
nodes to be up to two minor
versions older than the master.

00:08:36.049 --> 00:08:39.409
In the next challenge,
the final one--

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:41.100
probably the more crucial one--

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:43.390
are security fixes.

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:44.870
And obviously, the
sense of urgency

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:46.670
to roll out these
security features,

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:49.940
security patches is
higher, especially when

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:53.060
there are some critical
issues, like the one we just

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:54.590
experienced last December.

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:56.880
So you don't need
to lean forward to--

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.940
I know it's kind
of an eye chart.

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:03.350
But in this case, there
was a security issue

00:09:03.350 --> 00:09:05.720
with the Kubernetes API.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:09.080
We patched it, and we
quickly rolled it out

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:10.460
to the entire cluster.

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:13.410
So no action was actually
required from our customers.

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:16.040
If you used GKE, you
benefit from the rollout

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:17.750
of these patches.

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:20.150
In these cases-- in the
security issue cases--

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:23.780
GKE team will do their best to
identify, resolve, and patch

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:26.270
the entire fleet
and your clusters

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:30.750
as well to keep your
workloads safe and secure.

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:34.500
For GKE as a managed
Kubernetes service,

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:36.570
we strive to remove
that level of complexity

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:38.220
that I just described.

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:40.740
And how we deal with
all these challenges,

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.373
Josh is going to
give us some details.

00:09:43.373 --> 00:09:44.040
JOSH HOAK: Cool.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:49.790
So really, when I think about
release of Kubernetes in GKE,

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:54.680
I'm really talking about, how do
we get the open source software

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:57.520
into Google Kubernetes Engine?

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:00.770
And I see it as having,
like, five main phases.

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:04.220
So the first phase is the
open source release happens.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:06.220
And I'm going to [? lite ?]
a lot of stuff here.

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:08.620
But the open source release
is quite complicated.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:11.440
I mean, there's a whole process
of release qualification,

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:14.720
and you have to
write release notes.

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:16.660
Kubernetes has these
special interest groups

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:19.570
that are a sort of federated
way that features are created,

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:22.390
and all these people
have to get together.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:24.337
But from my perspective,
working on GKE,

00:10:24.337 --> 00:10:26.170
you kind of wait for
that process to finish.

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:28.810
And then-- boom-- they come
out with a new version.

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:33.880
Then we import that
version internally,

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:36.520
and we start applying our
patches on top of that.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:39.220
After that, then we
qualify the version.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:42.010
And this can be quite
a long period of time,

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:43.900
sometimes weeks or
months, depending

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:45.220
on what the version is.

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:48.430
And then, at some
point, we're like, OK,

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:50.750
it is time to release
a new version.

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:52.040
Let's cut a release candidate.

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:53.665
So we cut a release
candidate, and then

00:10:53.665 --> 00:10:57.710
that period is about,
roughly, one to two weeks.

00:10:57.710 --> 00:11:00.050
And then we roll it out
to production, of course,

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:01.932
assuming everything
works perfectly.

00:11:01.932 --> 00:11:03.640
And then once it rolls
out to production,

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:07.120
we actually have a
four-day rollout period.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:09.940
So we roll it out to a
couple locations first

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:13.270
and a couple locations next,
well, more than a couple.

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:16.090
And then we gradually
do all the locations.

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:18.500
And we try to put this
all in our release notes.

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:21.125
Now, of course, these days
might not happen sequentially.

00:11:21.125 --> 00:11:22.750
We might pause it if
we find an outage,

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:24.790
and then fix the outage
before we proceed on.

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:28.330
But this is sort of
the sequential order.

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:30.610
So what are we releasing?

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:32.415
When you see, oh,
we're releasing

00:11:32.415 --> 00:11:33.790
a new version of
Kubernetes, this

00:11:33.790 --> 00:11:36.240
is kind of what
Kobi was getting to.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:39.103
We have these four
different version numbers.

00:11:39.103 --> 00:11:40.270
So we got the minor version.

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:41.770
We have the Kubernetes patch.

00:11:41.770 --> 00:11:43.453
So those both come
from upstream.

00:11:43.453 --> 00:11:45.370
And then we have the
GKE-specific patch, which

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:48.782
is what we're adding on top.

00:11:48.782 --> 00:11:49.990
And what corresponds to that?

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:52.353
This is kind of our
identifier for the software.

00:11:52.353 --> 00:11:54.520
This is the collection of
software that we release--

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:58.150
the OS image, Kube API server,
Kubelet logging, whatever.

00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:01.930
So it's a very important number.

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:04.150
And remember, we
have two versions.

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:07.690
We have the master version,
and we have the node version.

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:10.000
And those are separate,
again, because of this shared

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:12.838
responsibility, where
we own the master,

00:12:12.838 --> 00:12:14.380
and we are sort of
in charge of that.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:17.170
I mean, you can upgrade your
master if you want on your own,

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:21.590
but we will do it eventually
for everyone, if you wait.

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:23.650
And then there's the
nodes, which you can opt in

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:24.640
to node auto-upgrades.

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:27.120
But ultimately, it's
your responsibility.

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:30.420
And we have to sort of
manage these things.

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:31.540
That gets into upgrades.

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:34.750
How do we upgrade the fleet for
both the masters and the nodes?

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:37.600
And Zack's going to talk
a little bit about that.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:38.500
ZACH LOAFMAN: Cool.

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:41.240
Thanks.

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:45.860
Most of the users of GKE,
[? i.e. you, ?] experience

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:49.490
our release process
during upgrades,

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:52.010
whether it's a master
upgrade, which we initiate,

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:54.680
or whether it's a node upgrade,
which you have the option

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:59.640
of initiating yourself
or managing through us.

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:01.790
So first, let's talk
about the masters.

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:03.560
Master upgrades are managed--

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:05.600
and like I mentioned--
typically initiated

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:08.150
as part of the GKE
release process.

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:10.160
Workloads running
on user nodes just

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:13.280
stay running during the
master upgrade process.

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:14.910
When we upgrade
the control plane,

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:19.230
we also upgrade all of our
managed add-ons as well.

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:22.220
So as you can see
in this picture,

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:26.910
the master is upgraded along
with the versions for Istio,

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:29.220
Knative, and, as I
mentioned before,

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:33.500
all those logging and monitoring
and metrics support add-ons,

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:35.720
all while your user
application is running.

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:40.120
How do we do it?

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:42.420
We upgrade zonal
masters in place

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:46.990
by creating a new master
with new software.

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:51.050
Then we shift the
disk and network over,

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:55.290
and we kill the old master.

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:59.100
We do this pattern
for zonal masters.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:02.460
For regional
masters, where there

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:06.810
are three HA masters running,
we do a very similar pattern,

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:10.020
where we spin up
one more master.

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:12.450
And then we kind of roll
through doing a rolling update

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:15.430
on the masters.

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:20.270
So node upgrades-- node upgrades
are a very similar process,

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:22.720
but they're more visible to you.

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:25.090
In GKE today, you
can actually choose

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:27.100
who manages the node upgrade.

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:30.800
And I'll get more into
that in a little bit.

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:33.520
But if you have node
auto-upgrade turned on,

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:37.920
you we will initiate the
node upgrades for you.

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:40.980
And if not, you will
initiate them yourself.

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:46.550
So how does a node upgrade work?

00:14:46.550 --> 00:14:50.870
Well, it's actually a very
similar rolling update process.

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:53.660
What we do is we create a
new node at the new version.

00:14:56.890 --> 00:15:01.170
We cordon off and
drain the old node.

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:07.520
The workload shifts
scheduling to the new node,

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:10.260
and then we kill
off the old node.

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:14.520
This actually isn't
exactly right.

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:17.220
What I just showed you was a
feature we have coming up soon,

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:19.530
called surge upgrades,
where we will actually

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:21.660
give you the capacity for
one more node as you're

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:23.430
doing the upgrade.

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:25.560
Currently, the way
the picture works

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:29.820
is, we cordon and
drain an existing node,

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:33.770
and then add that back in
after we've deleted it.

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:40.613
So over to Josh to talk
about the version lifecycle.

00:15:40.613 --> 00:15:41.280
JOSH HOAK: Cool.

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:44.220
So you might be wondering, why
do we have so many versions?

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:47.040
And part of that is just
the natural lifecycle

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:49.770
of the versions and
really how it relates

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:51.370
to this upgrade process.

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:55.050
So I'm going to first talk about
minor versions of Kubernetes,

00:15:55.050 --> 00:15:57.300
even though, in many
ways, the minor version

00:15:57.300 --> 00:15:58.560
is kind of the major version.

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:01.560
But it's sort of the feature
version of Kubernetes.

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:04.000
Because that's what
most people care about.

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:08.160
All right, so what does
it look like in my head,

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:10.350
and how do we sequence this?

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:12.330
Well, normally,
what happens is when

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:14.280
we release a new
minor version, there's

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:17.250
a period time when
it's a new version.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:19.320
And what that means is,
it's available for you

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:21.460
to choose to use.

00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:23.400
But it's not default,
and so you can

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:27.880
use this to use new features
for GPUs, or TPUs, or whatever.

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:30.322
At some point, after
about maybe a quarter,

00:16:30.322 --> 00:16:32.280
we decide to make that
the default. We're like,

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:35.070
OK, we're pretty
sure that this is

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:40.340
a solid enough version for the
entire fleet of GKE clusters.

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:44.292
And then when we shift over the
default to the next version,

00:16:44.292 --> 00:16:46.250
now we start to phase
out the old minor version

00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:47.660
of Kubernetes.

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:51.670
So now, that gets into upgrades.

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:52.750
When do we do upgrades?

00:16:52.750 --> 00:16:54.880
We do this in the
phase-out period.

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:58.000
So during that
third-period time is

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:00.100
when we start to do
the master upgrades.

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:02.700
And we upgrade
everything that was

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:04.450
the default, but is
no longer the default,

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:07.800
to the new default.
Sometime after

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:11.400
that, we upgrade the
nodes to the new node

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:14.339
version for those
customers that have

00:17:14.339 --> 00:17:17.540
the node auto-upgrade enabled.

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:19.319
And patches work
very, very similarly,

00:17:19.319 --> 00:17:21.930
except that the phase
is much, much shorter.

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:24.470
So typically, for
a patch, we're not

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:27.569
talking about the order of
weeks rather than quarters.

00:17:27.569 --> 00:17:32.360
So we release a new patch, and
then we migrate people over.

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:34.610
And it sort of happens
in rapid succession,

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:39.840
because patches are our bug
fixes and less invasive.

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:41.885
And that's about it for
the version lifecycle.

00:17:41.885 --> 00:17:43.260
So now, I'm going
to pass it over

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:46.435
to Kobi to talk about how
you can manage your risk.

00:17:46.435 --> 00:17:47.310
KOBI MAGNEZI: Thanks.

00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:52.280
So now that you have
visibility into kind

00:17:52.280 --> 00:17:57.050
of the behind-the-scene process
of how we release Kubernetes

00:17:57.050 --> 00:17:58.430
and the strategy
we have, I think

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:01.070
it's important to know what
controls you have today,

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:04.370
control that enable
you to manage risk

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:07.600
and also control the disruption.

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:09.970
I would say, the
first thing that

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:13.420
might be pretty obvious, but
transparency is key for us.

00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:15.340
And actually, we
want to make sure

00:18:15.340 --> 00:18:18.250
that we provide you with
visibility into our release

00:18:18.250 --> 00:18:21.490
scope, the schedule,
and the lifecycle.

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:24.670
Our release notes include
more than just change log.

00:18:24.670 --> 00:18:28.450
We actually publish information
about upcoming versions,

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:33.040
the lifecycle versions
that we are duplicating,

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:34.190
and even more than that.

00:18:34.190 --> 00:18:37.762
I think I saw someone taking a
picture before of their release

00:18:37.762 --> 00:18:38.970
cadence in different regions.

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:39.820
We actually publish it.

00:18:39.820 --> 00:18:41.290
That's part of
the release notes.

00:18:41.290 --> 00:18:42.790
So if you go into
the release notes,

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:46.510
you can see exactly the
four days rollout schedule

00:18:46.510 --> 00:18:49.830
and in which region we are
releasing a version first

00:18:49.830 --> 00:18:52.180
and kind of get more
information that you

00:18:52.180 --> 00:18:55.480
can use to plan your
Kubernetes clusters.

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:00.790
JOSH HOAK: Yeah, and as Kobi
said, picking a location

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:01.530
is important.

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:02.840
The day 1 zones happen first.

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:05.550
And typically, the big
outages-- if they happen, which,

00:19:05.550 --> 00:19:09.090
hopefully, they don't-- we catch
them in day 1 and day 2 zones

00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:09.915
and regions.

00:19:12.500 --> 00:19:14.407
Additionally, like
I was alluding to

00:19:14.407 --> 00:19:16.490
and maybe you figured out,
it's important to think

00:19:16.490 --> 00:19:18.770
a little bit about which
version you're picking.

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:21.230
If you want the latest and
greatest Kubernetes features

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:23.480
or Google Cloud
features that come

00:19:23.480 --> 00:19:26.987
with the new version
of Kubernetes on GKE,

00:19:26.987 --> 00:19:28.070
choose the latest version.

00:19:28.070 --> 00:19:32.518
But we are very careful about
only setting the default when

00:19:32.518 --> 00:19:34.310
we think it's the right
time, when we think

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:37.580
this is the version that
the majority of the fleet

00:19:37.580 --> 00:19:38.360
should be on.

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:47.050
ZACH LOAFMAN: While you're
thinking about the version,

00:19:47.050 --> 00:19:51.310
another configuration to think
about is node auto-upgrade.

00:19:51.310 --> 00:19:53.110
As I alluded to
earlier, either you

00:19:53.110 --> 00:19:55.360
can manage your node
upgrades yourself,

00:19:55.360 --> 00:19:57.520
or you can have us manage them.

00:19:57.520 --> 00:19:59.590
With node auto-upgrade
enabled, anytime

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:04.550
that we do a master
upgrade, shortly after,

00:20:04.550 --> 00:20:07.820
we will upgrade all
of your nodes as well.

00:20:07.820 --> 00:20:10.970
We highly recommend
you enable this.

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:13.880
If you don't, it's
your responsibility

00:20:13.880 --> 00:20:17.600
to stay within the two
minor version skew policy

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:18.230
that we have.

00:20:21.080 --> 00:20:24.302
The node auto-upgrade
feature is actually default

00:20:24.302 --> 00:20:26.510
now in the Cloud Console,
which is the example you'll

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:27.830
see on the right.

00:20:27.830 --> 00:20:31.610
And it will soon be the
default in G Cloud as well.

00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:40.967
So we take various automated
actions on our clusters.

00:20:40.967 --> 00:20:42.800
And we recognize that
some of those actions,

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:46.670
like node upgrades, might cause
disruptions to your workload

00:20:46.670 --> 00:20:49.700
or carry some risk
associated with them.

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:51.440
So if there are
times of day that

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:54.620
might make more sense for
you to perform upgrades,

00:20:54.620 --> 00:20:56.780
you can configure
maintenance windows

00:20:56.780 --> 00:21:01.545
to stop automated actions on
your cluster for those Windows.

00:21:01.545 --> 00:21:03.920
And there's an example for
how to configure a maintenance

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:06.130
window up here and
more documentation.

00:21:06.130 --> 00:21:12.080
And now, over to Kobi
to talk about customers.

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:13.910
KOBI MAGNEZI: So
with that in mind,

00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:18.220
I invite Daniel to share more
about their experience as GKE

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:20.850
customer and tell us
more about their product

00:21:20.850 --> 00:21:22.940
and their business needs.

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:24.910
DANIEL LORETO: Thank you, Kobi.

00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:26.090
My name is Daniel.

00:21:26.090 --> 00:21:28.180
I'm the head of
engineering at Virta.

00:21:28.180 --> 00:21:31.180
I wanted to share with
you how we use GKE

00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:34.295
or why we chose it as
our runtime platform.

00:21:34.295 --> 00:21:35.920
But first, let me
give you a little bit

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:38.140
of context about Virta.

00:21:38.140 --> 00:21:39.850
Virta is a company
that provides,

00:21:39.850 --> 00:21:43.180
essentially, continuous
remote care for diabetes.

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:46.450
And so we use technology
to be able to provide

00:21:46.450 --> 00:21:48.560
24/7 care to our patients.

00:21:48.560 --> 00:21:50.900
We have clinically-proven
results.

00:21:50.900 --> 00:21:53.770
And through technology, we
can scale that treatment

00:21:53.770 --> 00:21:57.110
to lots of patients.

00:21:57.110 --> 00:21:59.470
Providing care
through technology

00:21:59.470 --> 00:22:01.840
is a challenging area.

00:22:01.840 --> 00:22:04.420
We need to maintain a
doctor-patient relationship

00:22:04.420 --> 00:22:06.970
through technology
asynchronously.

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:10.240
Many of our customers
are very risk-averse.

00:22:10.240 --> 00:22:11.950
We're dealing with health care.

00:22:11.950 --> 00:22:14.030
People's lives and
health are at stake.

00:22:14.030 --> 00:22:19.550
And so downtime or a bug could
have serious adverse effects.

00:22:19.550 --> 00:22:22.540
And we're working in a very
heavily-regulated industry

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:24.040
with regulations, such as HIPAA.

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:33.190
And so part of why we choose
GKE as a runtime environment

00:22:33.190 --> 00:22:37.960
is because it makes some
of our concerns easier.

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:42.070
GCP is a high-trust
certified platform.

00:22:42.070 --> 00:22:44.890
That's an important
certification in health care.

00:22:44.890 --> 00:22:47.980
And a lot of those controls,
we're able to inherit.

00:22:47.980 --> 00:22:50.350
And we have a shared
responsibility model,

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:54.850
where we're able to meet the
necessary regulation, in part,

00:22:54.850 --> 00:22:58.780
thanks to the help
that Google provides.

00:22:58.780 --> 00:23:01.100
Security updates--
super-important for us.

00:23:01.100 --> 00:23:04.240
And the fact that Google
can do that for us takes

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:07.060
a lot of that burden away,
so that our engineering team

00:23:07.060 --> 00:23:09.110
doesn't have to worry about it.

00:23:09.110 --> 00:23:12.820
And making running a
GKE cluster easier,

00:23:12.820 --> 00:23:16.100
essentially, removes a
lot of operational cost.

00:23:16.100 --> 00:23:19.390
So our engineers can focus
on business needs as opposed

00:23:19.390 --> 00:23:23.910
to maintaining the
infrastructure.

00:23:23.910 --> 00:23:26.370
That said, there's
also challenges

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:29.820
we've experienced,
particularly around managing

00:23:29.820 --> 00:23:32.200
all the different GKE versions.

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:33.780
So for example,
sometimes we want

00:23:33.780 --> 00:23:37.110
to have the cutting-edge
version in certain clusters.

00:23:37.110 --> 00:23:39.210
So our developers
are experimenting

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:42.720
with the latest version,
but a more stable version

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:44.280
in production.

00:23:44.280 --> 00:23:48.000
Sometimes we're ready to
roll out a certain GKE

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:51.560
version to production, and so we
have a pre-production cluster.

00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:55.770
And we have to manage
that upgrade of versions

00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:57.850
all the way to production.

00:23:57.850 --> 00:24:01.080
And we want to be fairly
careful also with the windows

00:24:01.080 --> 00:24:01.950
in which we do this.

00:24:01.950 --> 00:24:06.390
So we mentioned earlier, there's
a maintenance window feature.

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:08.970
But today, that
allows you to specify

00:24:08.970 --> 00:24:12.420
four-hour windows in which
you want to load the updates.

00:24:12.420 --> 00:24:14.680
We love a little bit more
flexibility with that.

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:18.540
For example, avoiding
it during weekends.

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:20.550
Or sometimes we
assign a customer,

00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:22.840
and we're going to have
a big spike of patients.

00:24:22.840 --> 00:24:26.173
And so we'd like to avoid
upgrades during that window.

00:24:26.173 --> 00:24:27.840
And so back to Kobi,
who's going to talk

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:30.660
about how those challenges
relate to different customer

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:32.224
personas.

00:24:32.224 --> 00:24:33.224
KOBI MAGNEZI: Thank you.

00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:39.110
So we just heard from Virta,
and thanks for sharing

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:40.310
your experience with us.

00:24:40.310 --> 00:24:42.440
Evidently, one size
doesn't necessarily

00:24:42.440 --> 00:24:44.990
fit all, even though we're
talking about managed cloud

00:24:44.990 --> 00:24:46.460
service.

00:24:46.460 --> 00:24:48.390
Different customers
have different needs.

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:53.570
And I want to share with
you some other examples.

00:24:53.570 --> 00:24:55.340
So we heard about Virta.

00:24:55.340 --> 00:24:57.600
We actually created
some customer persona

00:24:57.600 --> 00:24:59.600
to represent different
needs from different type

00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:01.080
of customers.

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:02.630
So in this case,
customer A, they

00:25:02.630 --> 00:25:06.800
have a specific multi-weeks
certification process

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:07.803
for every release.

00:25:07.803 --> 00:25:09.470
And they have to go
through that process

00:25:09.470 --> 00:25:12.230
and certify that release
before they roll it out

00:25:12.230 --> 00:25:13.850
into production.

00:25:13.850 --> 00:25:15.250
They're using GKE
today, and they

00:25:15.250 --> 00:25:18.560
are looking to align their
internal certification

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:21.150
process with the GKE releases.

00:25:21.150 --> 00:25:24.240
Another example would
be this customer.

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:25.990
They upgrade today manually.

00:25:25.990 --> 00:25:29.310
So even though we have
the auto-upgrade option,

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:31.620
they decided to upgrade
manually, because they

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:33.165
want better control.

00:25:33.165 --> 00:25:35.540
And they want to make sure
that they understand what they

00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:37.260
are getting in every release.

00:25:37.260 --> 00:25:39.150
They are definitely
excited about the ability

00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:41.490
to test early
version of Kubernetes

00:25:41.490 --> 00:25:45.920
and get better time to
market with new capabilities.

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:49.790
And in this case, this type
of customer, customer C,

00:25:49.790 --> 00:25:51.500
they are pretty self-sufficient.

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:53.180
They know Kubernetes
pretty well.

00:25:53.180 --> 00:25:54.770
They feel very
comfortable with it.

00:25:54.770 --> 00:25:57.650
They want to adopt new
feature quickly and maybe even

00:25:57.650 --> 00:26:01.880
be on the bleeding edge
version of the Kubernetes.

00:26:01.880 --> 00:26:04.190
But they also want
the ability to manage

00:26:04.190 --> 00:26:07.580
different environments
and maybe have

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:11.040
different type of versions in
each one of these environments.

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:13.430
So with this example
in mind, we are

00:26:13.430 --> 00:26:17.000
excited to share with you some
of our upcoming capabilities

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:20.480
that will, one, provide you
more control and flexibility,

00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:22.780
and two, will allow us to
cater to different types

00:26:22.780 --> 00:26:23.937
of customers.

00:26:27.685 --> 00:26:30.060
ZACH LOAFMAN: An exciting
feature we have coming up in Q2

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:31.765
is release channels.

00:26:31.765 --> 00:26:34.140
Release channels will allow
you to subscribe your cluster

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:37.290
to releases that match your
release cadence and risk

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:41.490
profile, keeping the
masters and nodes together

00:26:41.490 --> 00:26:44.430
at the channel that's
just right for you.

00:26:44.430 --> 00:26:46.940
Our goal is that every
change is qualified

00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:49.800
in an earlier channel before
it moves to a later channel.

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:54.700
So for example, any change that
made it to this regular channel

00:26:54.700 --> 00:26:56.270
would have been
qualified previously

00:26:56.270 --> 00:26:58.490
in the rapid channel.

00:26:58.490 --> 00:27:00.635
Soon, we'll release
the rapid channel,

00:27:00.635 --> 00:27:02.885
where you can expect the
latest and greatest releases.

00:27:06.280 --> 00:27:10.480
Later this year, we will follow
with the regular channel--

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:13.600
bundled and qualified pieces
from the rapid channel--

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:15.670
with a stable
channel soon after.

00:27:15.670 --> 00:27:17.860
We expect that most
of you will want

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:19.390
the rapid or regular channels.

00:27:19.390 --> 00:27:22.630
But if you need our most
stable but least feature-full

00:27:22.630 --> 00:27:24.970
releases, the stable
channel is for you.

00:27:28.890 --> 00:27:30.420
There's a simple example here.

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:32.460
Soon, you'll be able to
create a release channel

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:36.270
cluster with as straightforward
a command line as just saying,

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:37.950
release channel rapid.

00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:41.430
And we'll follow with Cloud
Console changes later, too.

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:44.400
And you'll get a cluster whose
master nodes are maintained

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:50.440
at the newest release that we've
qualified internally for GKE.

00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:53.706
This will be available later
this quarter, hopefully.

00:27:57.173 --> 00:27:57.840
JOSH HOAK: Cool.

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:00.540
So this is something I'm
very passionate about.

00:28:00.540 --> 00:28:03.570
One of the things that I've
been working on is the release.

00:28:03.570 --> 00:28:10.267
And as part of release channels,
we want more granular releases,

00:28:10.267 --> 00:28:11.100
at least internally.

00:28:11.100 --> 00:28:13.715
And we've been making a lot of
new tooling and infrastructure.

00:28:13.715 --> 00:28:15.840
And some of that's provided
in this new open source

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:18.190
repository we created
called the Cluster Bundle.

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:19.732
So if you're curious
kind of like how

00:28:19.732 --> 00:28:21.240
we think about
releasing components,

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:23.852
definitely check it out.

00:28:23.852 --> 00:28:25.560
What is that going to
mean for customers?

00:28:25.560 --> 00:28:27.150
You won't see it
directly, but you'll

00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:29.460
see it in the form of
more stable releases,

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:32.670
improved time to market,
and better release nodes.

00:28:37.990 --> 00:28:39.990
KOBI MAGNEZI: So in
addition to release channels

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:42.600
we just discussed,
release channels

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:46.020
allow you to inform us
with your risk appetite

00:28:46.020 --> 00:28:48.630
and kind of the type
of version or release

00:28:48.630 --> 00:28:51.490
you want to subscribe
to your cluster to.

00:28:51.490 --> 00:28:54.930
We also want to support you in
managing your risk or your risk

00:28:54.930 --> 00:28:59.070
mitigation plans, and canary
cluster can help with that.

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:01.260
A canary cluster will
allow you to designate

00:29:01.260 --> 00:29:05.690
a cluster for canary or
staging test and blink it

00:29:05.690 --> 00:29:09.250
or map it with your production
cluster or clusters.

00:29:09.250 --> 00:29:12.600
In turn, what we will do is
GKE will sequence new version

00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:15.260
rollout to canary
clusters first.

00:29:15.260 --> 00:29:18.300
And we'll give you some time
to run your automate test,

00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:20.250
make sure that
everything works, nothing

00:29:20.250 --> 00:29:22.030
breaks with the new version.

00:29:22.030 --> 00:29:24.270
And once you greenlight
that version,

00:29:24.270 --> 00:29:28.045
we will roll out the new
version to production.

00:29:30.900 --> 00:29:35.580
The other thing we are going to
do as an alpha feature coming

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:38.070
up from product is enhanced
maintenance windows,

00:29:38.070 --> 00:29:39.985
which we just spoke about.

00:29:39.985 --> 00:29:41.610
So right now, with
maintenance windows,

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:45.810
you can specify four hours in
a day that we will respect,

00:29:45.810 --> 00:29:48.280
and we will trigger
maintenance events only

00:29:48.280 --> 00:29:49.590
in these four hours.

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:51.120
But we do hear from
other customers

00:29:51.120 --> 00:29:53.620
that there are other use cases
that they want us to support.

00:29:53.620 --> 00:29:57.397
For example, if you prefer to
avoid upgrade during weekends,

00:29:57.397 --> 00:29:58.980
because you a gaming
company, and most

00:29:58.980 --> 00:30:02.513
of the traffic you're getting
is actually during the weekend,

00:30:02.513 --> 00:30:04.680
you will be able to use
enhanced maintenance windows

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:07.860
and set the specific
days that you

00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:12.280
want us to avoid those upgrades
or those maintenance events.

00:30:12.280 --> 00:30:14.490
Other use case might
be you are a retailer,

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:17.640
and you want to make sure
that we don't disrupt you

00:30:17.640 --> 00:30:19.260
with any type of
maintenance events

00:30:19.260 --> 00:30:21.460
during Black Friday,
Cyber Monday.

00:30:21.460 --> 00:30:24.150
So you'll be able to
specify a range of dates

00:30:24.150 --> 00:30:25.170
that we will respect.

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:27.300
And we will avoid
maintenance windows

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:29.660
happening in these dates.

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:32.010
Now the last use
case is, let's say,

00:30:32.010 --> 00:30:33.630
you know about upcoming upgrade.

00:30:33.630 --> 00:30:36.540
But for some
internal reasons, you

00:30:36.540 --> 00:30:40.560
need more time to get ready
for that specific upgrade.

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:43.620
We will allow you to
postpone or snooze

00:30:43.620 --> 00:30:45.952
the upgrade for a
specific amount of time

00:30:45.952 --> 00:30:47.910
to kind of give you more
flexibility in getting

00:30:47.910 --> 00:30:51.430
ready for consuming the upgrade.

00:30:51.430 --> 00:30:54.750
So just bring all of this
together, because we definitely

00:30:54.750 --> 00:30:57.810
spoke about a lot of
things in this session,

00:30:57.810 --> 00:31:02.070
our goal is to keep your cluster
up and running, up to date,

00:31:02.070 --> 00:31:05.850
while delivering a
non-disruptive experience

00:31:05.850 --> 00:31:09.900
for your customers
and for your clusters.

00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:13.950
We aim to provide a
hassle-free managed service.

00:31:13.950 --> 00:31:14.970
That's only on one side.

00:31:14.970 --> 00:31:16.387
And the other side
is we're trying

00:31:16.387 --> 00:31:19.912
to balance that with give you
more controls for flexibility.

00:31:19.912 --> 00:31:21.870
If you are interested in
any one of the feature

00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:24.040
that I just mentioned
that we spoke about today,

00:31:24.040 --> 00:31:26.580
please follow that
link over there.

00:31:26.580 --> 00:31:30.300
And let us know that you want to
join one of the alpha programs

00:31:30.300 --> 00:31:31.430
that I mentioned.

00:31:34.930 --> 00:31:36.520
Your feedback is
highly appreciated

00:31:36.520 --> 00:31:37.900
as with any other sessions.

00:31:37.900 --> 00:31:40.300
So after this session and
after we're done with the Q&amp;A,

00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:42.910
please take a moment
to go through the app

00:31:42.910 --> 00:31:46.330
and share your experience
with this session.

